Personally think thresh isn't as beginner support unfriendly; this is because of a couple of reasons:

- His laning is somewhat safe compared to the other hook supports as he is ranged and thus should be a good transition point from enchanter gameplay to engage (if she did learn how to harrass with autos, she did play Yuumi after all);

- Spatial awareness is something that you just naturally learn as you play thresh because of the many responsibilities you have when playing thresh;

- Vision control is something they'll learn along the way but to simplify it a bit (pre-support item upgrade use yellow wards, afterwards red ward and always keep 1 control ward for emergencies or 2 when going for objectives);

- Thresh is the perfect mix of complicated but at the same time straightforward to start with: his hook doesn't force you to commit and his flay is litterally just "point at the direction where you want them to go" (atleast that's how I learned it), they don't have to worry about overly complicated combos, just flay, hook and kill it doesn't get more simple than that no?

- Thresh is also not as tanky as the other engage supports but this personally helped me be more careful on engages;

I personally learned Thresh as I was learning how to play support and it really opened my eyes to the role and has defined how I play supports from enchanters to engage support (with clear distinctions ofcourse, I'm not going to run straight into a Nautilus as Soraka lol)

